NAME
       auth, sudo, passwd_set - authentication

SYNOPSIS
       int cervus_auth(const char *user, const char *password);
       int cervus_sudo(void);
       int cervus_passwd_set(const char *user, const char *password);

DESCRIPTION
       Authentication lives in the kernel rather than in a library, so
       a program cannot decide for itself that a password was correct.

       auth checks a password against the stored SHA-256 hash. sudo
       asks whether the caller may act as root. passwd_set changes a
       password, which the kernel allows for your own account, or for
       any account when the caller is root.

       Consequently there is no path to a root shell without a
       password.

RETURN VALUE
       0 when the check succeeds, negative otherwise.

ERRORS
       EPERM    the password is wrong, or the caller may not do this
       ENOENT   no such user

SEE ALSO
       su(1), sudo(1), passwd(1), login(1), getpid(2)
